y session data will be synced, including the `brain` directory and the `history.jsonl` file. Lastly, a session index will be generated from the conversation summaries database.\n\n\nClaude and Codex sessions will be synced using a robust file synchronization tool, handling exclusions for temporary and lock files to maintain data integrity. A README file will be generated to document the session data structure and its update process. A symbolic link to the `sessions` directory will be created for easier access.\nGit status will be checked and changes added/committed with a descriptive message if new sessions are detected. Branch information will be retrieved dynamically for the commit message.\n\n\nThe new playbook and its symlink to the repository root should be added. The playbook will be placed in `playbooks/sync-ai-sessions.yml`, with a symlink at the root, `sync-ai-sessions.yml -> playbooks/sync-ai-sessions.yml`, following the existing repository structure.\n\nIt's necessary to verify if the playbook can be executed using either `ansible-playbook sync-ai-sessions.yml` or `ansible-playbook playbooks/sync-ai-sessions.yml`, and how `playbook_dir` is resolved.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"ansible -i localhost, localhost -m debug -a \"msg={{ playbook_dir }}\"","Cwd":"/home/koby/LINUX","IsDaemon":false,"RequestedTerminalID":"","RunPersistent":false,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Testing playbook_dir resolution","toolSummary":"Test playbook_dir resolution"}}]}
